# Test naming

A test name says what someone using the unit is trying to do, in what
situation, and what they get. It is not vague — it is behaviour, in words a
reviewer understands without opening the implementation.

## Shape in this repo

{{TEST_NAME_SHAPE}}

If a file already uses a simpler shape and the task is not a rewrite, keep its
structure and improve only the wording.

## Rules

### 1. Start from intent, not mechanism

| Prefer | Avoid |
|---|---|
| When I open the alert list | When the hook is rendered for the first time |
| Then the list refreshes | Then listAlerts was called twice |
| Then the requested page and sort are used | Then the repository receives the built query |

### 2. Translate the technical check into its meaning

When a test asserts a mechanism (a call, a count, an argument), name it after
the reason that mechanism matters to the user or caller. The assertion stays
precise; the label states the behaviour.

### 3. Be concrete

"Then the close dialog stays open", "Then my current filters are applied" —
not "Then it works", "Then success", "Then data updates".

### 4. Keep internal names out

No function, hook, store field, repository method, mock or callback names in a
label — unless the word is part of the domain language the user shares
(`alert`, `report`, `draft`, `next page`).

### 5. Short enough to scan

One idea per label, product language, no justification clause. It must still
read well in the runner's failure output.

## Not every unit has a user

A pure function, a mapper or a low-level contract has a caller, not a user.
Name its tests from the caller's point of view in plain behaviour terms
("rejects a title longer than the limit", "maps an absent date to none") rather
than inventing a fake product sentence.

## Rewriting a file

1. Read each scenario and its assertions first.
2. Name the user's goal at the outermost group.
3. Name the situation or action at the nested level.
4. Name the outcome at the case.
5. Change labels only: logic, assertions and coverage stay as they were unless
   the task asked for more.

## Checklist

- [ ] Every label states behaviour, not implementation.
- [ ] Someone new to the file understands each scenario from its labels.
- [ ] No internal identifiers unless they are domain words.
- [ ] Existing structure kept unless a rewrite was requested.
- [ ] Labels still precise enough to locate a failure.
